Figure 623: Boot Partition Overview

![This diagram illustrates the process of reading data from a Boot Partition into a Boot Memory Buffer located in Host Memory, likely as part of a system boot or firmware loading mechanism. Here’s a breakdown of the components and relationships shown: --- ### 🖥️ Left Side: Host Memory - A large green rectangle labeled “Host Memory” represents the main memory space of the host system (e.g., RAM). - Inside it, a smaller region is labeled “Boot Memory Buffer” — this is where data from the boot partition will be copied. - Below the Host Memory block, an arrow points to it with the label “Boot Memory Buffer Address”, indicating the memory address where the buffer resides. --- ### 💾 Right Side: Boot Partition - A light blue rectangle labeled “Boot Partition” represents a storage region (e.g., on an eMMC, NVMe, or SD card) that contains boot-related data. - Within this partition: - A darker blue shaded area is labeled “Data to be Read”. - This area is bounded by two annotations: - “Boot Partition Read Offset”: Indicates the starting address or position within the boot partition from which reading begins. - “Boot Partition Read Size”: Indicates how much data (size) is being read from that offset. --- ### 🔗 Data Flow - A red arrow points from the “Data to be Read” region in the Boot Partition to the “Boot Memory Buffer” in Host Memory. - This signifies that data is being read from the boot partition (starting at the specified offset, for the specified size) and copied into the boot memory buffer in host memory. --- ### 🎯 Purpose This visual likely depicts a low-level operation during system initialization — such as loading a bootloader, kernel, or firmware from non-volatile storage (boot partition) into RAM (host memory) so it can be executed. The diagram emphasizes the key parameters needed for this operation: - Where to start reading (offset) - How much to read (size) - Where to put the data (buffer address) --- ### ✅ Summary > The diagram shows how a specific block of data (“Data to be Read”) is extracted from a designated region (“Data to be Read”) within a “Boot Partition” (using “Read Offset” and “Read Size”) and transferred into a “Boot Memory Buffer” in “Host Memory” at a known address — a common step in embedded systems or OS boot processes. This is a clear, conceptual representation of a memory-to-storage read operation with explicit addressing and sizing controls.]

To read the contents of a Boot Partition using NVMe properties, the host allocates a Boot Partition Memory Buffer in host memory for the controller to copy contents from a Boot Partition. The host initializes the Boot Partition Memory Buffer Base Address. The host sets the Boot Partition ID, Boot Partition Read Size, and Boot Partition Read Offset to initiate the Boot Partition read operation. The host may continue reading from the Boot Partition until the entire Boot Partition has been read.

A portion of the Boot Partition may be read by the host any time the NVM subsystem is powered (i.e., whether or not CC.EN is set to '1'). The host shall not modify transport specific properties (described in the applicable NVMe Transport binding specification), reset, or shutdown the controller while a Boot Partition read is in progress.

To read data from a Boot Partition, the host follows these steps:

1. Initialize the transport (e.g., PCIe link), if necessary;

2. Determine if Boot Partitions are supported by the controller (CAP.BPS);

3. Determine which Boot Partition is active (BPINFO.ABPID) and the size of the Boot Partition (BPINFO.BPSZ);

4. Allocate a physically contiguous memory buffer in the host to store the contents of a Boot Partition;

5. Initialize the address (BPMBL.BMBBA) into the memory buffer where the contents should be copied;

6. If no Boot Partition read is in progress (i.e., the Boot Read Status (BPINFO.BRS) field is not set to 01b), then initiate the transfer of data from a Boot Partition by writing to the Boot Partition Read Select (BPRSEL) property. This includes setting the Boot Partition Identifier (BPRSEL.BPID) field, the Boot Partition Read Size (BPRSEL.BPRSZ) field, and the Boot Partition Read Offset (BPRSEL.BPROF) field;

7. Wait for the controller to set the BPINFO.BRS field to 01b while the controller is transferring the Boot Partition contents to indicate that a Boot Partition read operation is in progress; and

8. Wait for the controller to completely transfer the requested portion of the Boot Partition, indicated in the status field (BPINFO.BRS). If BPINFO.BRS is set to 10b, the requested Boot Partition data has been transferred to the Boot Partition Memory Buffer. If BPINFO.BRS is set to 11b, there was an error transferring the requested Boot Partition data and the host may request the Boot Partition data again.

